The first surviving Valuation Revision Book records James on Inishrush plot 29 in 1860–1863 at exactly 19 acres, 2 roods and 10 perches (19.5625 statute acres). Compared with Adam's 1833 tithe holding of about 17.82 statute acres, the later farm was only 1.7425 statute acres, or about 9.8 per cent, larger. This strongly supports inheritance or continuation of substantially the same family holding, although parcel identity is not proved because Adam's tithe entry has no map number and the surveys differed in date, purpose and measurement. 

The Valuation Revision Books then show occupation of plot 29 passing:

* from James Glasgow;
* to Rose Anne Glasgow, inserted with an 1884 revision annotation;
* to a later Adam Glasgow, inserted with an 1893 annotation.

The holding remained 19 acres, 2 roods and 10 perches. These later records establish continuity of plot 29 but do not themselves state the relationships between James, Rose Anne and the later Adam. PRONI Valuation Revision Books (https://apps.proni.gov.uk/Val12B/Search.aspx), VAL/12/B/34/10A–E, Inishrush plot 29 (accessed 17 July 2026).

