The possible identification of Robert has a chronological caution. The only adult female in Archibald's 1800 household was aged 16–25. If she was Mary and Robert was born close to 1790, she would have been at the extreme young end—or too young—to be his mother. Robert may have been born later than the 1850 census implies; Archibald may have had an earlier wife; the boy may have been another relative; or one of the reported ages may be wrong.

Private family details withheld.

The valuation entries establish succession in occupation of the farm but do not themselves state the family relationships among the occupants.

Accordingly, neither the 1847 Barkley report nor Rose Anne's succession to plot 29 provides evidence identifying this older James's wife or children.
Further research
Useful records for this James include:

* records identifying his wife;
* marriage or church records establishing his marriage;
* records explicitly identifying Henry Glasgow's parents;
* estate, lease and valuation material clarifying transmission of the Inishrush farm;
* records identifying the relationships among the later occupants of plot 29.

Research into the child reported in the 1847 Barkley letter belongs primarily with the younger James Glasgow and Rosanna Barkley rather than with this profile.

First Kilrea Presbyterian session minutes survive for 1825–1852 under PRONI reference MIC1P/87, and the Drimbolg Reformed Presbyterian session or discipline book survives for 1809–1859 under MIC1C/15. Both cover the period of the reported 1846 event and remain relevant unread sources for the younger James and Barkley research.

The death of James Glasgow was registered on 12 December 1878 in Magherafelt, aged 84. GRONI civil death index (https://geni.nidirect.gov.uk/search/death), James Glasgow, 12 December 1878, aged 84, Magherafelt registration district, D/1878/179/1022/7/138.

The matching name, occupation and parish, together with the sequential dates, identify the bridegroom in both entries as the same James Glasgow.
Children
James's known children were:

* Helen Glasgow, daughter of Bessie Whyte;
* Jonet Glasgow, daughter of Bessie Whyte;
* Robert Glasgow, son of Agnes Duncan; “Scotland, Births and Baptisms, 1564–1950,” database, FamilySearch, Robert Glasgow, baptised 5 May 1655, Edinburgh (https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:VRSV-T1T), son of James Glasgow and Agnes Duncan.

* Jean Glasgow, recorded in 1728;
* William Glasgow, recorded in 1730;
* Helen or Hellen Glasgow, recorded in 1732; and
* Christian Glasgow, recorded in 1740.

The public Emerald Ancestors index independently lists the 1728 Jean, 1730 William and 1732 Helen entries in County Antrim, although its public results do not expose the parents or full register details. Emerald Ancestors, County Antrim birth index (https://www.emeraldancestors.com/northern-ireland-ancestry-search-birth-list?County=Antrim&Forenames=&Surname=Gillis&brideforenames=&bridesurname=&civildistrict=&searchtype=), entries for Jean, William and Helen Glasgow.

* Thomas Glasgow, deceased, in Quodquen;
* John Miller, described as an executor;
* W[illia]m Glasgow, apparently owing £8 Scots;
* Isobell Fisher in Glasfurde;
* James Gibson in Quodquen;
* a probable William Wilsone;
* Master John Carmichael, commissary of Lanark;
* an unidentified cautioner for Bessie Glasgow.

Thomas Glasgow and W[illia]m Glasgow may have belonged to the wider Glasgow family at Quodquen, but the testament does not state their relationship to John.
