She is also attached as the uncertain mother of:

* David Glasgow, born about 1824 at Coleraine;
* James Irvin Glasgow, born about 1828 in Ireland;
* Daniel T. Glasgow, born about 1835 in Ireland.

The proposed relationships are based on chronology, locality and the absence of evidence for another wife or partner of Daniel. Only Robert's baptism explicitly names Margaret.

James was described in an 1853 death notice as the third son of Daniel Glasgow of Kilrea. Len Swindley, "Glasgow Genealogy Notes, Counties Tyrone & Londonderry, Northern Ireland 1840–69" (https://www.cotyroneireland.com/surnames/glasgow.html), transcription of a notice published in the Londonderry Sentinel, 18 February 1853, describing James Glasgow as the third son of Daniel Glasgow of Kilrea (accessed 16 July 2026).

She appointed Hiram Walworth of Plattsburgh as executor and gave him authority to sell or reinvest her property as necessary to carry out the provisions of the will. 
Relatives Named in the Will
Nancy explicitly identified several relatives:

* Robert Anderson, deceased, whom she called her nephew;
* Dinah Anderson, widow of Robert Anderson;
* Robert Wallace Anderson, whom Nancy also called her nephew, and whose mother was Dinah Anderson;
* Margaret (Dysart) Kennedy of West Troy, New York, whom Nancy called her niece. 

Nancy directed her executor to retain $500 in trust for Robert Wallace Anderson until he reached the age of 21. If he died before that age, the money and accumulated interest were to pass to his mother Dinah. 

She left $200 to Dinah Anderson and $200 to Margaret Kennedy. The residue of her estate was left to Dinah Anderson or her legal representatives. 

Other legacies included:

* $100 to the Woman's Union Missionary Society of America for Heathen Lands;
* $50 to the trustees of the Peristrome Presbyterian Church of Plattsburgh;
* $50 to Ann Cavanagh, wife of John Cavanagh of Plattsburgh;
* $50 to Alexander Weir of Plattsburgh;
* $50 collectively to Carrie B. Walworth, Graham H. Walworth, Clarence B. Walworth, Mary E. Walworth and Lottie Place of St. Albans, Vermont. 
Death and Probate
A transcription of Riverside Cemetery at Plattsburgh records a Nancy Glasgow as dying on 31 October 1891. This is a strong candidate for the testator, although the original gravestone or cemetery register has not yet been examined. Northern New York Tombstone Transcription Project, Riverside Cemetery, Plattsburgh, A–K (https://nnytombstoneproject.nygenweb.net/clinton/plattsburgh/riverside_a_k.htm), entry for Nancy Glasgow, reporting death 31 October 1891.

Probate Citation and Extended Family
The 1892 probate citation provides considerable evidence for Nancy's wider family network, although it does not state the exact relationship between Nancy and most of the people named.

Among those cited were:

* James G. Glasgow, Ann and Lizzie, children of John Glasgow, formerly of Baladonly, County Antrim, Ireland;
** Probable match John Glasgow (abt.1801-1895), whose marriage names his father as John Glasgow, farmer; identification of that father with John Glasgow (1751-abt.1852) remains unproved.
* James Glasgow, Mary and Ann, children of Alexander Glasgow, formerly of Rasharkin, County Antrim, Ireland;
** Best match Alexander Glasgow (abt.1811-1888) who has children James, Mary and Ann. 
* Mary Jane and Ann, children of Robert Glasgow;
** Robert Glasgow (abt.1779-abt.1852) or Robert Glasgow Sr (abt.1811-abt.1888), altho neither are a good fit, maybe Robert Glasgow (abt.1849-)? 
* Robert Dysart of Rasharkin and James Dysart;
* Sallie, daughter of Jane Catherine (Glasglow) Dysart, of Ballymena;
* Mary Jane (Dysart) Hampton, another daughter of Jane Dysart, believed to be living in Canterbury, New Zealand;
* Martha Dysart and Elizabeth Weir, both living in Canterbury, New Zealand;
* Margaret (Dysart) Kennedy of West Troy, New York;
* David Anderson, James Anderson and Samuel Anderson of Gortahar, County Antrim;
* the unnamed children of Ann McClelland;
* the unnamed children of Mary Thompson. 

The citation describes these people collectively within proceedings involving Nancy's "heirs or next of kin", but it does not state that John, Alexander and Robert Glasgow were Nancy's brothers, nor does it identify Nancy as a daughter of any of them. Their precise relationships remain under investigation. 

The Alexander Glasgow identified in the notice as formerly of Rasharkin may be connected with the documented Glasgow family of Lisnagaver, Rasharkin parish. However, this notice alone does not establish that he was Nancy's brother. The combined will and Irish household evidence supports that relationship as probable.

In the 1870 U.S. census, Nancy Glascow, aged 50 and born in Ireland, was living in Plattsburgh, Clinton County, New York, with Delia Walworth. The household connection is consistent with Nancy’s later appointment of Hiram Walworth as executor and bequests to Walworth children; it establishes a close Plattsburgh association, not Irish Glasgow kinship. The age, birthplace, residence and spelling variation strongly fit this Nancy, but the census does not name her parents or establish her relationship to Alexander Glasgow. FamilySearch (https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:M8FF-ZQD), U.S. census, 1870, Nancy Glascow, Plattsburgh, Clinton County, New York, record M8FF-ZQD, accessed 16 September 2026.

His apprenticeship independently identifies his father as James Glasgow, weaver. This description connects Robert to the household of the Edinburgh weaver rather than to James Glasgow, the distinct James baptised at Inveresk in 1626.
Robert Meane
Robert's master was recorded as Robert Meane, merchant. Further research into Meane's business, burgess records, correspondence and apprentices may help identify Robert Glasgow's later career.

The abbreviation “mt.” in the published apprentice register describes Meane as a merchant. It does not establish that Robert Glasgow had completed his apprenticeship or had himself become a merchant.
Unproved 1672 correspondence
A Robert Glasgow sent several letters from Scotland to Secretary Sir Joseph Williamson during 1672. Some were written under the direction of Robert Dickson and concerned military, maritime and parliamentary news.

No inspected record establishes that the correspondent was the sixteen-year-old apprentice baptised in 1655. The shared name and Edinburgh association are insufficient to prove identity, particularly because the correspondent appears to have occupied a position involving regular governmental communications.

The following references may concern a different Robert Glasgow and should not be presented as events in this man's biography without additional identifying evidence:

* a report dated 2 March 1672 concerning ten companies ordered by the Council;
* a letter dated 6 April 1672 written by command of Robert Dickson;
* a report dated 8 May 1672 concerning the ship Speedwell and its prizes;
* a letter dated 22 May 1672 concerning the Earl of Dunfermline; and
* reports sent during July 1672 concerning proceedings in the Scottish Parliament.

Useful identifying details would include the correspondent's occupation, employer, age, relatives, residence, signature, or later career.
Further research
Useful remaining records include:

* the original 1655 baptism entry;
* the original apprenticeship indenture or incorporation records;
* records concerning Robert Meane and his business;
* evidence that Robert completed his apprenticeship;
* Edinburgh burgess, marriage and burial records;
* testaments naming Robert or his parents; and
* the original 1672 correspondence attributed to Robert Glasgow.
Supplied parish index comparison
The supplied Scotland’s People index records ROBERT GLASGOW on 11/05/1655 at Edinburgh, with parent text “JAMES GLASGOW/AGNES DUNCAN FR1546 (FR1546)”. Scotland’s People, Old Parish Registers, births and baptisms (https://www.scotlandspeople.gov.uk/), exact surname Glasgow through 1700, index text supplied 10 September 2026: ROBERT GLASGOW, 11/05/1655, JAMES GLASGOW/AGNES DUNCAN FR1546 (FR1546), indexed gender M, Edinburgh, parish 685 / 1, reference 50 / 570. Original register image not inspected for this review.

